e in order to quantitate samples not  a big deal especially if you have  automation you just set up the curve and  you run it and then you run your samples  afterwards and that's what I did here I  made up a curve in soybean oil and then  ran my my hemp oil right after that but  I spiked and I also ran some hemp oil on  spiked now you'll probably notice that  butane and isobutane aren't on my list  and I know that those are common  solvents that are looked for for labs  doing this type of testing and the only  reason is  they would work just fine by the method  I'm going to show you but I just didn't  have the right kind of setup to spike  those gasses into them into my materials  accurately the column that I chose is a  porous layer open tubular column capella  to plot and I chose it because if you do  use butane and isobutane this column  will retain them and separate them from  all the other analytes of interest and I  use GCMs in full scan mode ok so these  are my calibration curves out of my  soybean oil and I got pretty good  linearity as you can see here I was  actually getting such good sensitivity  with this method that at about 70 ppm I  was starting to see a little bit of  overload on some of the compounds and  the cutoff I know for organ it varies  depending on the psalm you're looking  for but generally if you can see down to  10 ppm that's going to be fine for most  of these which spme has no problem with  it's an enrichment technique very very  sensitive so in my spike 10 boil I  spiked it at 10 ppm and for spiked  replicates my recoveries we're above 80%  for all the compounds and my RS DS were  very good I got very good  reproducibility they were below 10% now  hexane is missing missing from my bar  chart here that is because I detected  hexane in my unspeak temp extract at 59  parts per million so obviously I know  what they use to make that extract with  okay terpenes now this technique  solid-phase microextraction SPME can  also be used for doing terpenes either  qualitative profiling or quantitative  analysis now in this application here we  have some cannabis that we have obtained  through ni da and we did a tour peen  profile on the material just on dry  flower material by exposing n SPME fiber  to the headspace  there's different types of SPME fibers  available just the one that you select  depends on what you want to do so I  chose one that would be very good for  getting a low molecular weight and very  volatile compounds so I chose the one  that's described here this DVB car box  in PDMS  with a very very simple extraction I  just exposed the fiber to the headspace  over the dry plant material for 20  minutes after I had warm the sample up  for 30 minutes to get it equilibrate and  I was able to identify 45 different  proteins in that plant material now I  did that using a combination of GCMs and  retention indices also to SPME can be  used in a field sampling mode and these  samplers can be taken out in the field  they're available in little sealed  holder so you do your sampling in the  field and when you bring the fiber back  inside of the assembly it it's sealed  off with a septum and then they can be  shipped back to the lab and analyze and  so we did a something called a cannabis  boot camp out in Portland Oregon a  couple years ago at chalice farms which  is a dispensary there and we use these  spme field samplers to look at terpene  profiles from three different varieties  of cannabis that they took out of did  they had there at their facility and  that's me on the far right and I'm  sampling one type and I know two helpers  that were sampling two other types and  the picture below the second picture  just shows that we just all we did was  just exposed the sqv field samplers to  the cannabis flowers for about five  minutes then we shipped the samplers  back to Milla pour Sigma in Bellefonte  Pennsylvania and about two weeks later i  desorbed them into a GCMs system this  was the first variety that we sampled  hippie Crippler the second variety  Slimer and the third variety which was  super Orange Crush and you can see we  got very nice européenne profiles and  we're able to see some differences  between them so this could be a pretty  useful tool if say you wanted to do some  terpene sampling for qualitative reasons  perhaps out in the fields for  identification of a strain or other  reasons  now quantitation of terpenes using us  Timmy we also worked up a quantitative  method and this method was they had some  different parameters than our  qualitative methods and we chose to look  at three specific terpenes that are of  interest pining limonene a little and we  studied them from the cannabis variety  that we had in house that we had  obtained from NIDA we spiked what we  found was when we did the qualitative  determination we then analyzed our  cannabis quantitatively and we found  that the candidate the terpene content  on it was very very very low okay we  don't know how old the cannabis was we  don't know what variety it was so in  order to make it closer to the varieties  that are being sold in dispensaries now  we had to spike the cannabis with known  levels of terpenes to bring it up to  relevant levels and that's what we did  so we spiked their cannabis and then  quantitated it against a curve that we  had prepared and extracted by SPME and  then we compare that to that spiked  cannabis sample that we prepared by a  conventional extraction method many labs  use an ethanol extraction which is what  I'm showing on this slide to produce an  extract that they just shoot into a GCF  ID to look for terpenes the extract is a  beautiful green color as you can see and  it also turns the GC liner a nice green  color after about 10 runs we found so  what we did was we took this spike to  extract we extracted it with with the  ethanol method I'm showing bail and we  also extracted it using our headspace  spme method and what we found was that  the results were very close to each  other  reproducibility was good for both  techniques and the accuracy was good for  both techniques the difference is is  that with SPME we did not have to use  any organic solvent it was also a  hands-off technique we set up the  samples and walked away there were  basically two steps just putting the  cannabis in the file  we found adding a little water helps  putting it up on the instrument walking  away where as you can see with the  ethanol extraction there's essentially  three to four steps that are required  and you still end up with a green  extract that kind of mucks up your  instrument  so why GCMs for terpenes here's another  thing - a lot of labs use fi d GCMs is  better why well if you use fi d you're  not going to be able to know for sure  what you're looking at is what you think  it is and what do I mean by that well  let's take a look at this peak at  nineteen point eight one minute set of  the terpene peak by GCMs nice peak if we  look at the spectrum on the back half of  it compared to the front there are some  similar ions but you can see that  they're very different the reason is is  these are two terpenes or co loading  with each other  looking at that peak you would never  know okay but if by looking at spectra  on the front versus the back you can  tell that they're two essentially two  things coming off together so what I did  was I subtracted the front the back from  the front and I was able to identify it  as this compound here that I'm showing  on the on the bottom left so GCMs helps  to identify for sure these Peaks as  specific terpenes now is spectral  searching like I just showed you a  sufficient by itself to identify terpene  not entirely for example let's say we  have two terpene Peaks one at twenty  five point eight five minutes and one  they've looked at twenty five point nine  seven minutes let's look at their  spectra this is my first peak at twenty  five point eight five minutes and it is  alpha yep a 